 6 rewrites the body of `CreateAgentFromRunner` (three steps, three `NextPhase` values, three phase registrations), but the only testing requirement (Req 7) covers `pkg/prompts`. The phase-wiring contract (`NextPhase` string must match a registered `Phase.Name`) is a dispatch-registry boundary and is untested here; it is only covered by prompt 3's AC3 chain test. That bounded split is defensible, but a typo in the wiring would surface one prompt late. Add one cheap assertion.\n\nOld:\n```\n   Keep the `EnvContext: envContext` on every step — that is how the `service_repo.<service>` mapping reaches the planning prompt. Keep `CreateAgent` and `CreateAgentProvider` calling `CreateAgentFromRunner`; only its body changes. Do **not** add the verdict gate, do **not** change the dispatch table, and do **not** add a parameter — those are the last prompt's work.\n```\nNew:\n```\n   Keep the `EnvContext: envContext` on every step — that is how the `service_repo.<service>` mapping reaches the planning prompt. Keep `CreateAgent` and `CreateAgentProvider` calling `CreateAgentFromRunner`; only its body changes. Add one cheap assertion in `pkg/factory/factory_test.go` that `CreateAgentFromRunner` registers three phases named `planning` / `execution` / `ai_review` whose steps carry the matching `NextPhase` values, so a typo in the phase wiring fails in this prompt rather than the next. Do **not** add the verdict gate, do **not** change the dispatch table, and do **not** add a parameter — those are the last prompt's work.\n```\n\n**Rec 2 — Extra `branch:` frontmatter field.**\nThe DoD allows only `spec`, `status`, `created`, `issue` in inbox frontmatter; dark-factory adds `branch` on approve. All three sibling prompts carry it, so this is generator-wide, not prompt-2-specific.\n\nOld:\n```\ncreated: \"2026-09-28T01:40:00Z\"\nbranch: dark-factory/diagnose-observed-defect-with-verdict-gate\n---\n```\nNew:\n```\ncreated: \"2026-09-28T01:40:00Z\"\n---\n```\n\n**Rec 3 — Unknown-phase fallback branch has no production consumer.**\n`BuildInstructions`'s non-canonical-phase branch is unreachable in production and its only test exists to cover that branch. The prompt already justifies it (open question 2), so this is informational. If kept, the dedicated one-row `DescribeTable` is overkill.\n\nOld:\n```\n   Then add a `DescribeTable` over `prompts.BuildInstructions(domain.TaskPhase(\"bogus\"))` asserting the one-element fallback returns only the `output-format` instruction. Keep every `It`/`Entry` body short by passing the required strings as `Entry` arguments rather than writing one spec per phase.\n```\nNew:\n```\n   Then add a single `It` asserting that `prompts.BuildInstructions(domain.TaskPhase(\"bogus\"))` returns only the `output-format` instruction — a one-row `DescribeTable` is unnecessary. Keep every `It`/`Entry` body short by passing the required strings as `Entry` arguments rather than writing one spec per phase.\n```\n\n**Rec 4 — `<objective>` exceeds 1–3 sentences and names files.**\nThe third sentence names `pkg/prompts/` and the three `*.md` filenames, which the DoD reserves for `<requirements>` (\"not implementation-level\"). End state is achievable without the paths.\n\nOld:\n```\nGive each diagnoser phase instructions of its own. Today every phase shares one scaffold prompt that says nothing about defect diagnosis, so planning never resolves a service, execution never observes the running system, and review never checks the verdict. After this change `pkg/prompts/` carries `planning.md`, `execution.md` and `ai_review.md`, each embedded and selected by phase, and `pkg/factory` builds three distinct steps from them. The generic scaffold prompt is deleted, so no phase can fall back to it.\n```\nNew:\n```\nGive each diagnoser phase instructions of its own. Today every phase shares one scaffold prompt that says nothing about defect diagnosis, so planning never resolves a service, execution never observes the running system, and review never checks the verdict. After this change each phase is selected by its own embedded instructions and the factory builds three distinct steps from them; the generic scaffold prompt is deleted, so no phase can fall back to it.\n```\n\n**Rec 5 — Requirement 2 does not name the allowed `confidence` set explicitly.**\nThe three contract values appear only inside the copied template line (`confidence: High | Medium | Low`). Naming them removes any chance the model emits a fourth value (the schema-drift failure mode).\n\nOld:\n```\n   - give a cause that is supported only by reading source a `confidence` below `High`, and reserve `High` for a cause the live reads show;\n```\nNew:\n```\n   - give a cause that is supported only by reading source a `confidence` below `High`, reserve `High` for a cause the live reads show, and use only the three contract values `High`, `Medium` or `Low`;\n```\n\n## Strengths\n- **Verification is genuinely container-executable and non-vacuous**: file-existence checks, per-phase `grep -F` string assertions, a distinct-script-name cap (`-le 4`), the scaffold-sentence absence check, the pod-log negative, the `NewAgentStep` count, and an explicit ≥80% coverage assertion. Nothing depends on `git`, Docker, or a cluster.\n- **Boundary awareness is explicit**: the `<context>` calls out spec 001's three-way consistency test and pins the exact four allowlisted script names the new prompt text may mention — precisely the allowlist boundary this change crosses.\n- **All six \"verified facts\" in `<context>` are accurate** against the live tree (`prompts.go`, `workflow.md`, `prompts_test.go`, `factory.go`, `output-format.md`, the `claudelib` API), and every inlined external symbol (`claudelib.Instructions`/`Instruction`/`String`, `NewAgentStep`, `AgentStepConfig` fields, `agentlib.NewAgent`/`NewPhase`, `domain.TaskPhase*`) greps to a real declaration at the cited version.\n- **Sibling entry points handled**: the prompt names both `CreateAgentFromRunner` call