{"duration_api_ms":3548763,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"d0a8f161-dbd4-4f10-88f7-931e909fc308","total_cost_usd":17.260083,"usage":{"input_tokens":261,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19072,"cache_read_input_tokens":247296,"output_tokens":7873,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"deepseek-v4-flash[1m]":{"inputTokens":1069,"outputTokens":14327,"cacheReadInputTokens":841344,"cacheCreationInputTokens":54016,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.121792,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"deepseek-v4-flash[1m]","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"unknown"},"deepseek-v4-flash-max[1m]":{"inputTokens":8828,"outputTokens":222719,"cacheReadInputTokens":17041152,"cacheCreationInputTokens":320896,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":16.138291,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"deepseek-v4-flash-max[1m]","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"unknown"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","origin":{"kind":"task-notification"},"subagent_stats":{"spawned":4,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":4,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":1,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":4,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{"dark-factory:prompt-creator":1,"dark-factory:prompt-auditor":3}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":6,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Three prompt files were generated and all three audits returned. Reporting below.\n\nOne process note first: the prompt-creator agent was killed twice by `API Error: Connection lost before a response was produced`, both times at the moment of emitting a ~17 KB single `Write` call, with zero files written. It succeeded on the third attempt after being told to write each file incrementally (Write frontmatter+summary, then Edit to append each section). That workaround is worth keeping.\n\n## Generated Prompts\n\n**Files created** (all in `prompts/`, the inbox):\n\n| File | Lines | Covers |\n|---|---|---|\n| `1-spec-002-verdict-gate.md` | 474 | AC4–AC7 — heading rule, gate rules a–k / a–j, line guard, stale-`## Failure` removal |\n| `2-spec-002-per-phase-prompts.md` | 261 | AC8 — `pkg/prompts/{planning,execution,ai_review}.md` |\n| `3-spec-002-wiring-dispatch-and-docs.md` | 294 | AC2, AC3, AC9 — dispatch, phase chain, README, CHANGELOG |\n\nExecution order 1 → 2 → 3. All three carry `make precommit` in `<verification>` (AC1).\n\n**Key decisions:**\n\n1. New package `pkg/verdict` with a pinned exported API: `NewGate(envContext, cloneRoot)`, `(*Gate).Evaluate` (rules a–j), `(*Gate).Review` (a–j again, then the ai_review line guard), `CheckLineGuard`, `PrefixMessage`, `NewPlanningStep` / `NewExecutionStep(inner, gate)` / `NewAIReviewStep(inner, gate)`.\n2. `Gate` is a concrete struct, not an interface — every test drives the real deterministic gate, so an interface would have no second implementation. Raised as an open question.\n3. Section removal is a filter over `md.Sections`, never string surgery; the gate performs it on every \"removed\" outcome.\n4. Prefixing happens exactly once, in the step wrapper, so model-passthrough envelopes and gate messages share one path. `Decision.Message` stays raw.\n5. `Evaluate` vs `Review` splits rule k's meaning by phase: `Evaluate`'s `Done` = advance to ai_review; `Review`'s `Done` = line guard passed, complete.\n6. Prompt 2 changes the *body* of `CreateAgentFromRunner` (three per-phase steps) so the repo stays compiling after `workflow.md` is deleted; prompt 3 adds the parameter, gate wrapping and dispatch. Flagged as a deviation from the spec's decomposition table.\n7. Prompt 3 owns the only `## Unreleased` entry; prompts 1 and 2 state explicitly that they must not add one, because AC9 greps for it.\n8. AC10 (the operator rung-1 run) is excluded from every `<verification>`; mentioned once in prompt 3's `<context>` with no commands.\n9. Reason strings for every rule are pinned in a table in prompt 1, so tests assert them character-for-character.\n\n**Verification work done before writing:** grepped `github.com/bborbe/agent@v0.89.0` for `Step`, `Markdown`/`Section`/`FindSection`, `TaskFrontmatter.String`, `Agent.Run`'s in-process phase walk, `claude.AgentStepConfig`, `NewAgentProvider`'s error text, `delivery.NewPassthroughContentGenerator`, and the `agentmocks.ClaudeRunner` API. No `defect-diagnose` constant exists upstream, so prompt 3 declares one locally. It also ran two throwaway programs (since deleted) to confirm that `yaml.v3` parses `file:line: pkg/x/y.go:42` as a single key, that a block scalar yields a value containing `\\n`, that `ParseMarkdown` splits at every column-0 `## ` line, and how the passthrough generator splices `## Failure`. It reported reverting a `go.mod` side effect — **I verified this: `gopkg.in/yaml.v3 v3.0.1` is still marked `// indirect`.**\n\n**Open questions embedded as comments** — 7 in prompt 1, 6 in prompt 2, 8 in prompt 3. The load-bearing ones: pinned messages for the section-missing / no-block / unparseable rows where AC4 says \"naming the field\" but no field exists; whether the line guard quotes the path or `path:line` (read literally from the docs); `Gate` as a concrete struct; keeping the name `BuildInstructions` with a phase parameter; a silent one-element fallback for an unknown phase rather than an error path; and the new tests constructing `service_repo.<service>` keys in a local `envContext` — the only way to exercise rules h and i, and a direct tension with the spec's Non-goal that a reviewer should confirm.\n\n**Also relevant:** the daemon is executing spec 001 concurrently — `001-spec-001-http-observation-scripts.md` is already complete, and `002-`/`003-` are in `prompts/in-progress/`. Spec 002's prompts depend on spec 001 being *merged*, not merely generated.\n\n## Audit Findings\n\n### prompts/1-spec-002-verdict-gate.md\n\n**Score: 8/10 — Good. No Critical issues.**\n\n**DoD checklist:** all six sections present; `<summary>` 10 plain-language bullets; `<requirements>` numbered 1–14 with pinned signatures and message strings; `<verification>` runnable; no bare `git` commands anywhere (matters — `.dark-factory.log` shows `hideGit=true hideGitSource=arg`); no operator-only commands; correct inbox location and filename pattern; `status: draft`. One flag: frontmatter carries `branch:`, outside the allowed inbox set — but the archived spec-001 prompt carries it too, so it is likely daemon-stamped.\n\n**Code references:** every framework symbol verified against `agent@v0.89.0` in the module cache — `Step`, `Result`, `Markdown`/`Section`/`FindSection` (including the correct assertion that no `RemoveSection` exists), `TaskFrontmatter.String`, `claude.AgentStepConfig`'s field list, the `ReplaceSection`-on-done / no-section-on-envelope behaviour, `Agent.Run`, `AgentResultInfo`'s seven fields, `NewPassthroughContentGenerator`, the `agentmocks.ClaudeRunner` methods, and the `errors.Errorf`→`Error()` identity. **Zero hallucinated symbols.**\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\n- **R1 — inaccurate `dupl` claim (line 343).** `.golangci.yml` excludes `dupl` for `_test.go`, so it does not analyse test files. Replace: `` reuse them everywhere; factor them into helpers so no `It` body is a copy of another (`funlen`, `gocognit` and `nestif` do apply to test files; `dupl` is excluded for `_test.go` in `.golangci.yml`, but factor the fixtures anyway so the tables stay readable). ``\n- **R2 — pinned reason strings are not on the page the prompt calls \"authoritative\".** `docs/verdict-gate.md` carries `no line cited; evidence:`, `evidence cites no live read`, `service %q does not resolve`, the three line-guard reasons and the heading-rule message — but none of `verdict section missing`, `verdict yaml block missing`, `verdict yaml does not parse`, `field %q contains a newline`, `confidence %q is not one of High, Medium, Low`, `field %q is missing or empty`, `file:line is not a repo-relative <path>:<positive line>`, `defect_id mismatch: …`, `repo %q does not match mapped repo %q`. Lines 36 and 117 overstate the verbatim claim; either put the strings on the page or name them prompt-authored.\n- **R3 — internal contradiction, `<constraints>` vs requirements 7/9 (line 428).** The constraint forbids a `service_repo.` entry in \"Config, code, **tests** or docs\", but requirement 7 mandates the fixture `{\"service_repo.checkout\": \"bborbe/foo\"}` and requirement 9 the key `service_repo.agent-task-controller` — both required by spec AC5. Prompt 3 has the carve-out; prompt 1 does not. Change \"**Do NOT add** any `service_repo.` mapping entry to this repo's Config, code, tests or docs\" to \"**Do NOT commit** any `service_repo.` mapping entry to this repo's Config, non-test code, or docs … The one exception is the fixtures in requirements 7 and 9, which construct `service_repo.<service>` keys in their own local `envContext` to exercise rules h and i — that is simulated operator input, not committed mapping data.\"\n- **R4 — over-broad negative grep (line 468).** `! grep -rn 'agent/repos' pkg/verdict/` matches explanatory comments, and requirement 11 (line 394) itself uses the literal `agent/repos/` — so an agent that documents why it avoids the path fails its own verification. Reword line 394 to `<agent dir>/repos/`, and optionally add `grep -qF 't.TempDir()' pkg/verdict/lineguard_test.go`.\n- **R5 — AC5's heading-injection row is under-asserted (line 377).** Requirement 9 asserts only the gate message and `PrefixMessage` safety; AC5 additionally requires the *written task* assertions (the set of column-0 `#` lines, the column-0 fence lines). Route that row through the real `delivery.NewPassthroughContentGenerator()` the way requirement 10 does.\n- **R6 — `NewGate` returns a concrete `*Gate` (lines 249, 274).** Deviates from `go-patterns.md`'s \"constructor returns interface\". The prompt documents the rationale and raises it as open question #7, so it is defensible — but the reviewer must decide it before approving.\n- **R7 — `branch:` in inbox frontmatter (line 5).** Outside the allowed set; most likely daemon-stamped.\n- **R8 — consider referencing `docs/creating-claude-agents.md`** in `<context>`, or say why not. Low priority: it covers the fork/deploy layer and cites stale paths (`lib/claude`, `agent/claude`, `make buca`).\n- **R9 — state the BSD license header requirement** for the new `.go` files in requirement 1. `addlicense` adds it automatically, but every existing file carries it, so naming it makes the diff deterministic.\n\n**Strengths:** every framework symbol real and verified; boundary-crossing contract tests are exemplary (requirement 11 builds a real throwaway clone with `t.TempDir()`, exercises symlink escape on both a file and a parent directory, and asserts the escaping sentinel is untouched; requirement 10 drives passthrough messages through the real content generator and asserts on the delivered body including the `## Failure` fence); correctly container-scoped; YAGNI-disciplined; pinned API keeps prompts 1 and 3 consistent.\n\n### prompts/2-spec-002-per-phase-prompts.md\n\n**Score: 8/10 — Good. No Critical issues.**\n\n**DoD checklist:** all six sections present; `<summary>` 9 plain-language bullets; `<requirements>` 8 items with exact paths and `//go:embed` blocks; `<verification>` runnable; no `git` commands anywhere in the file; no operator-only commands; correct inbox location and filename; `status: draft`. One flag: extra `branch:` frontmatter field (generator-wide, not prompt-2-specific).\n\n**Failure-mode coverage:** mapped row by row against the spec's table — \"no read path shows the misbehaviour\" → Req 2; invented repo/`defect_id`/no live read → Reqs 1–2; unmapped service → Req 1 (exact, case-sensitive); clone failure → Req 1; endpoint unreachable → Req 2; pod dies between phases → Req 3. Binary-side rows correctly deferred to prompt 1.\n\n**Code references:** all verified — `BuildInstructions()` and its embeds, `workflow.md:1`'s scaffold sentence, `prompts_test.go`'s three assertions, `CreateAgentFromRunner` at `factory.go:107-124`, `claudelib.Instructions`/`Instruction.String`, `claude.BuildPrompt`, `NewAgentStep`/`AgentStepConfig`, `agentlib.NewAgent`/`NewPhase`, `domain.TaskPhase*`. `BuildInstructions` has exactly three call sites repo-wide, all updated.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\n- **Rec 1 — requirement 6 changes production code with no test requirement in this prompt.** Req 6 rewrites `CreateAgentFromRunner`'s body (three steps, three `NextPhase` values, three phase registrations), but the only testing requirement covers `pkg/prompts`. The phase-wiring contract is a dispatch-registry boundary untested here; a typo surfaces one prompt late. Append to Req 6: \"Add one cheap assertion in `pkg/factory/factory_test.go` that `CreateAgentFromRunner` registers three phases named `planning` / `execution` / `ai_review` whose steps carry the matching `NextPhase` values, so a typo in the phase wiring fails in this prompt rather than the next.\"\n- **Rec 2 — remove the extra `branch:` frontmatter field.**\n- **Rec 3 — the unknown-phase fallback branch has no production consumer.** `CreateAgentFromRunner` only asks for the three canonical phases, and Req 7 adds a test solely to cover the unreachable branch. Replace the `DescribeTable` with a single `It` asserting `prompts.BuildInstructions(domain.TaskPhase(\"bogus\"))` returns only the `output-format` instruction.\n- **Rec 4 — `<objective>` exceeds 1–3 sentences and names files.** Drop \"After this change `pkg/prompts/` carries `planning.md`, `execution.md` and `ai_review.md`, each embedded and selected by phase\" in favour of \"After this change each phase is selected by its own embedded instructions\".\n- **Rec 5 — requirement 2 does not name the allowed `confidence` set explicitly.** The three values appear only inside the copied template line. Append to the bullet: \"…and use only the three contract values `High`, `Medium` or `Low`\".\n\n**Strengths:** verification genuinely container-executable and non-vacuous (file-existence checks, per-phase `grep -F` assertions, a distinct-script-name cap, the scaffold-sentence absence check, the pod-log negative, the `NewAgentStep` count, an explicit ≥80% coverage assertion); boundary awareness explicit — `<context>` pins the exact four allowlisted script names spec 001's three-way consistency test scans for; all six \"verified facts\" in `<context>` accurate against the live tree; both `CreateAgentFromRunner` call sites named and the dispatch table/signature fenced off so prompt 3's seam extension is not pre-empted.\n\n### prompts/3-spec-002-wiring-dispatch-and-docs.md\n\n**Score: 7/10 — Good. One blocking defect.**\n\n**DoD checklist:** all six sections present; `<summary>` 8 plain-language bullets; `<requirements>` numbered 0–10; `<verification>` runnable; no `git` anywhere; no operator-only commands (AC10 explicitly fenced off at line 64); correct inbox location and filename; `status: draft`; extra `branch:` field flagged as minor/tolerated.\n\n**Critical issue — `agentlib.TaskTypeLLM` is simultaneously forbidden in `pkg/factory/` and required in the new tests.** Three requirements and one verification line disagree:\n\n- Requirement 3 (line 175): \"The map has exactly three entries. `agentlib.TaskTypeLLM` must not appear anywhere in `pkg/factory/`.\"\n- Requirement 4 (line 177): \"Add one spec asserting `agentlib.TaskTypeLLM` now misses with `unknown task_type`.\"\n- Requirement 5 (line 183): \"`Get(ctx, agentlib.TaskTypeLLM)` returns an error whose message contains `unknown task_type`.\"\n- Verification (line 268): `! grep -q 'agentlib.TaskTypeLLM' pkg/factory/`\n\nThe new test files live under `pkg/factory/` and requirements 4–5 require them to reference the constant, so the verification target contains the very string it forbids. The auditor verified the grep semantics on this image: `grep` is **ugrep 7.8.4**, which searches a directory recursively even without `-r`, so the line evaluates to `! 0` → **fails**, making `<verification>` unpassable. On a GNU-grep image it would error on the directory (exit 2) → `! 2` → **pass for the wrong reason**, a false-positive verification pass that never enforced requirement 3. Either way it is unsatisfiable.\n\n**Fix:** scope both to the production file — requirement 3 to \"must not appear in `pkg/factory/factory.go` (the new tests reference it deliberately, to assert it is now rejected)\" and the verification line to `! grep -q 'agentlib.TaskTypeLLM' pkg/factory/factory.go`. While there, rewrite the now-stale doc comment at `pkg/factory/factory.go:126-132` (\"TaskTypeLLM routes to the existing 3-phase domain agent.\"). Alternative: reword requirements 4–5 to use the literal `agentlib.TaskType(\"llm\")` — faithful to AC2's `Get(\"llm\")` — and leave requirement 3's wording alone.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\n1. **Requirement 8 is factually wrong about the file state.** `CHANGELOG.md` already has an `## Unreleased` section with two bullets from spec 001. \"Add an `## Unreleased` section above `## v0.0.0` with exactly one bullet\" can be read as *replace it*, silently deleting two shipped entries — and the verification grep cannot detect that loss. Change to \"Append exactly one bullet to the existing `## Unreleased` section (do not replace the section or its existing bullets)\", and fix `<context>` line 42 likewise.\n2. **Requirement 4 miscounts the specs that change.** It says \"the two asserting the accepted-types list\"; `factory_test.go` has exactly one (line 84-86, `[healthcheck llm oauth-probe]`).\n3. **Requirement 6 drops the spec's two-prompt assertion for cases (e) and (f).** AC3's preamble requires cases (b), (d), (e) and (f) all assert \"the fake runner captured exactly two prompts, the second containing `scripts/prometheus-query.sh`\". Requirement 6 states this only for (b) and (d); (e) and (f) assert only `phase: execution`. The prompt's own open-question note 3 claims all four do.\n4. **`agentDir` is joined unresolved while the runner resolves it.** Requirement 1 uses `filepath.Join(string(agentDir), \"repos\")`, but `claude-runner.go:170-173` calls `AgentDir.Resolve` (tilde expansion) first. A `~`-prefixed `-agent-dir` would give the gate a literal-tilde clone root that never matches where `repo-clone.sh` wrote. Latent, not active — the default (`\"agent\"`) and the rung-1 run (`$(pwd)/agent`) are fine. Suggest documenting that `-agent-dir` must be absolute or cwd-relative.\n5. **Frontmatter carries `branch`** — likely generator behaviour rather than author error.\n6. **State the new package's import path** (`github.com/bborbe/defect-diagnoser-agent/pkg/verdict`) in requirement 1. Low priority.\n\n**Strengths:** every code reference accurate (all signatures, the two-call-site claim, the sorted accepted-types list, the `Validate` regex, the doc/heading anchors); sibling entry points handled explicitly — both the local-CLI path (`CreateAgent`) and the Kafka path (`CreateAgentProvider`) updated, with untouched callers called out as must-keep-compiling; container-autonomy clean and self-aware; the boundary-crossing contract test is present (`TaskTypeDefectDiagnose.Validate(ctx)` against `agentlib`'s `^[a-z0-9-]+$` / 63-char contract); failure-mode-driven details pinned (`trigger_count: 1` / `max_triggers: 3` rationale, the `RunReturns` default envelope, `dupl`-aware helper factoring).\n\n---\n\n**Spec transition:** skipped. `DARK_FACTORY_MANAGED=true` is set, so this ran in Mode 1 — the host-side generator finalizes `approved → prompted` after this container exits. The spec is currently at `status: generating`. (The `dark-factory` CLI is not present in this container; running `spec mark-prompted` here would fail with exit 127.)\n\nPer the command's instructions I did not retry, rewrite, or merge any prompt based on the findings above — the human reviewer owns that decision.\n\n## Improvements\n\n- [GLOBAL] **Large single `Write` calls kill the connection.** The prompt-creator died twice with `API Error: Connection lost before a response was produced`, both times at the moment of emitting a ~17 KB file write, producing zero files. Instructing it to write incrementally (Write frontmatter+`<summary>`, then Edit to append each section, ≤~4 KB per call) made it succeed immediately. This should be a standing rule in the prompt-creator agent definition for any multi-prompt generation, since 15–20 KB prompt files are the normal output size — the failure is silent (no file, no error artifact) and costs a full ~15-minute context-rebuild per retry.\n- [GLOBAL] **A resumed agent is not a retried agent.** `SendMessage` to a failed agent's ID preserves its context, so the two failures cost ~30 minutes of re-exploration only because I did not resume after the first one. Worth stating in the run-prompt / generate-prompts command docs: on a subagent API error, resume by ID rather than re-invoking.\n- [GUIDE] `dark-factory:prompt-auditor` should state the grep dialect it assumes. Audit 3's Critical finding hinges on `grep -q <pattern> <dir>` recursing under **ugrep 7.8.4** (this image) versus erroring under GNU grep — a verification line that fails on one and vacuously passes on the other. The auditor did verify this on the image, which is exactly right, but the guide should make \"confirm the image's grep dialect before scoring a directory-targeted grep\" an explicit step so it is not rediscovered per run.\n- [PROMPT] The generated prompts carry `branch:` in the inbox frontmatter, which all three auditors flagged as outside the DoD's allowed set (`spec`, `status`, `created`, `issue`). The spec-001 prompts carry it too, so the generator emits it consistently — either the DoD should list `branch` as allowed or the generator should stop emitting it. Currently it produces a finding on every single audit, which is noise.\n- [GUIDE] The Ginkgo suite-file boilerplate (`time.Local = time.UTC`, `format.TruncatedDiff = false`, 60 s suite timeout, `RunSpecs`) is now duplicated across `pkg/scripts`, `pkg/factory`, `pkg/prompts` and `pkg/verdict`. Spec 001's prompt inlined it verbatim; spec 002's prompts reference `pkg/factory/factory_suite_test.go` as the shape instead. A short `go-ginkgo-suite-file.md` in the coding docs would remove the last reason to inline it and make the two specs' prompts consistent.\n- [PROMPT] `docs/verdict-gate.md` is described in prompt 1 as carrying the reason strings \"verbatim\", but the pinned per-rule message formats for rules a, b, d, e, g and i exist only in the prompt. The spec says \"A rule change updates that page in the same change\", so the page should carry the exact strings — otherwise the next rule change has to diff a prompt to find them.","ttft_ms":1973,"type":"result","duration_ms":660677,"uuid":"43ea200d-67c8-4843-8a1f-102ff1a6efba","ttft_stream_ms":1447,"time_to_request_ms":75,"first_content_frame_ms":1447,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":2}
